{"jawbone_id":"6a9deb436bd0819196ab3ec694e294c2","title":"Hourly H(s)H Synthesis, Predictions & Papers","prompt":"CURRENT TEAM MISSION — SYNTHESIZE THE FINITE-CORE BUILD, PROMOTE ONLY FROZEN RESULTS, EXTRACT TIGHT PREDICTIONS, AND DRAFT PAPERS

Use the Reason with SAT/H(s)H skill and current team artifacts to maintain the compact source-grounded H(s)H synthesis while serving as the integration lane for the live finite-core discrimination program.

CORE OBJECTIVE
Each hourly run must integrate one narrow advance in the chain:
empirical Minkowski elements → radial/UI construction → finite-core history → H(s)H parametrization → resolving/readout → transport/deformation/nesting → Interbraid/Electrogravity → inherited-domain readouts.
The immediate choke point is finite-core/interface architecture. Track competing constructions without harmonizing them prematurely: full B^3-type normal core, B^2-type material support/subbundle, S^2/boundary carrier, finite resolving-intersection thickness, or a layered object in which these are bulk/support/boundary/readout views of one structure.

TEAM INTEGRATION
At the start of each run, inspect the latest available outputs/checkpoints from the forward-build, geometry/solver, covariance/representation, archive/provenance, outsider/orthogonal, and blind-audit lanes where accessible. Distinguish firsthand artifacts from handoff summaries. Do not manufacture consensus.

For each candidate construction, maintain a comparison ledger with:
- literal object/domain and dimension;
- canonical state and minimum data required;
- what ᚼ and ᚼᚼ mean in that representation;
- allowed perturbations and residual channels;
- boundary/interface/readout data;
- transport/deformation/history support;
- centerline/worldline limiting map;
- nesting behavior;
- topology support, including whether genuine open-Brunnian/string-link structure can be carried;
- representation invariants versus slice/coordinate artifacts;
- hidden degrees of freedom or underdetermination;
- equivalence maps to rival candidates;
- failure and freeze conditions.

PROMOTION RULE
A feature is not promoted merely because it resembles historical SAT, matches a particle datum, or appears in a polished equation roundup. Favor geometry that is required by the construction and residuals that survive admissible normalization/representation changes. Preserve contradictions explicitly.

TIGHT-PREDICTION PIPELINE
As soon as a frozen or nearly frozen construction implies a measurable relation with minimal auxiliary assumptions, create or update a PREDICTION LEDGER. Rank candidate predictions by:
1. precommitment before target comparison;
2. fewest free parameters/corrections;
3. dependence on frozen geometry rather than fitted constants;
4. ability to distinguish competing finite-core architectures;
5. precision and accessible independent data;
6. cross-domain reuse of the same parameter or invariant;
7. clear falsification condition.

For every prediction candidate record: ID; derivation source; assumptions; exact equation; predicted value/range/scaling/invariant; units; observable/readout map; comparator dataset/measurement not used in derivation; uncertainty; rival-model/candidate contrast; falsification condition; historical SAT occurrence only after provenance check; status UNFROZEN/CANDIDATE/FROZEN/TESTED/FAILED.

Do not use legacy B=3/(4π), 0.246 rad, mass ladders, Z3/Q≤3, 24-cell lattice, Kerr identity, or old scorecard formulas as premises unless independently regenerated by the current frozen construction. If regenerated independently, flag for Morrow/GitKeeper provenance review before claiming novelty or prediction priority.

PAPER PIPELINE — START EARLY, KEEP CLAIMS NARROW
Do not wait for a finished TOE. Whenever there is a bounded result with a stable derivation plus either (a) a clean mathematical construction/result, (b) a discriminator/no-go/equivalence theorem candidate, or (c) one or more genuinely precommitted empirical predictions, create or update a paper draft/skeleton.

For each paper candidate maintain:
- paper ID and working title;
- one-sentence claim;
- claim maturity;
- exact model/representation scope and epistemic boundary;
- definitions and mathematical setup;
- derivation/results;
- prediction/test where applicable;
- standard-math/physics comparison without interpretive overreach;
- provenance/prior-art section fed by archive lane;
- falsification/failure conditions;
- limitations/open dependencies;
- figures/tables/code needed;
- reviewer attack list;
- next condition required for submission-quality draft.
Prefer several narrow papers to one omnibus document. Distinguish source-grounded prose from generated connective text.

GOOD EARLY PAPER CLASSES TO WATCH FOR, WITHOUT ASSUMING THEY WILL SUCCEED
- finite-core object hierarchy and limiting maps;
- equivalence/non-equivalence of bulk, material-support, boundary, and resolving-intersection representations;
- canonical normalization and residual-grain calculus using ᚼ/ᚼᚼ;
- second-variation/fluctuation operator of the selected finite-core action and its spectral consequences;
- open-Brunnian/string-link topology for finite worldtubes;
- a specific tight empirical prediction emerging without target fitting;
- a falsification/null-constraint paper if a current H(s)H construction is cleanly ruled out.

SOURCE DISCIPLINE
Continue the existing source-precedence hierarchy and sequential reading rules. Use indexes as maps, not source substitutes. Keep H(s)H a representation/model/calculational grammar, not an assertion about inaccessible physical reality. Preserve the SPHERE4QC deferred cursor unless needed.

OUTPUT EACH RUN
Be concise but operational. Report:
1. one synthesis advance;
2. current finite-core comparison change;
3. any invariant/residual promoted, demoted, or still unresolved;
4. new/updated tight prediction candidates with status;
5. new/updated paper drafts with paper ID and maturity;
6. provenance or external-evidence dependencies awaiting other lanes;
7. one exact next team handoff or freeze decision.
If nothing is mature enough for a prediction or paper yet, state the blocking dependency rather than inventing one.","schedule":"BEGIN:VEVENT
