[L13] \section*{Introduction}

[L15] Human intuition is fundamentally three-dimensional. Our sensory and cognitive structures are adapted to 3D space and 1D time. Yet, nature presents itself as fundamentally four-dimensional: spacetime is not 3D evolving in time — it is \textbf{fully 4D from the start}.

[L17] To theorize rigorously in 4D requires a conceptual shift:
[L18] \begin{itemize}
[L19]     \item Objects are extended in time as well as in space.
[L20]     \item Dynamics are replaced by 4D geometry.
[L21]     \item Motion is interpreted as structure.
[L22]     \item Stability and binding arise from topological constraints.
[L23] \end{itemize}

[L25] This primer outlines the core rules and mindset shifts necessary to think and theorize natively in 4D.

[L27] \section*{1. 4D Objects: Worldtubes, Not Worldlines}

[L29] \textbf{Rule 1}: 
[L30] \begin{quote}
[L31] A 4D object is not something that ``exists over time.'' It \textit{is} its 4D extension — a worldtube, not a point tracing a worldline.
[L32] \end{quote}

[L34] \textbf{Rule 2}:
[L35] \begin{quote}
[L36] Everything you think of as an ``event'' is a cross-section of a 4D object.
[L37] \end{quote}

[L39] Particles are not points moving through space; they are extended 4D structures whose ``states'' at a given time are slices through their worldtubes.

[L41] \section*{2. Vibrations and Dynamics in 4D}

[L43] \textbf{Rule 3}:
[L44] \begin{quote}
[L45] Dynamics are just 4D shapes, not processes. A wave is a 4D spiral.
[L46] \end{quote}

[L48] A vibrating filament is a hyperhelical 4D object. Its vibration is encoded in its 4D geometry — not as motion over time, but as a built-in coiled structure.

[L50] \section*{3. Topology Over Geometry}

[L52] \textbf{Rule 4}:
[L53] \begin{quote}
[L54] In 4D, linking is more fundamental than shape.
[L55] \end{quote}

[L57] 4D structures are constrained by topology:
[L58] \begin{itemize}
[L59]     \item \textbf{Linking}, \textbf{knotting}, \textbf{winding} — not just geometric shape — determine physical properties.
[L60]     \item Energy, mass, and binding emerge from topological strain.
[L61] \end{itemize}

[L63] \section*{4. Avoid 3D Intuition Traps}

[L65] \textbf{Rule 5}:
[L66] \begin{quote}
[L67] If you find yourself ``evolving'' something through time, you are slipping back into 3D intuition.
[L68] \end{quote}

[L70] \begin{itemize}
[L71]     \item Avoid thinking of particles as points evolving in time.
[L72]     \item Think of the full 4D form — the sculpture, not the movie.
[L73] \end{itemize}

[L75] \section*{5. Phase and Symmetry in 4D}

[L77] \textbf{Rule 6}:
[L78] \begin{quote}
[L79] Phase is a 4D angle — it defines orientation in 4D vibrational structures.
[L80] \end{quote}

[L82] Phase relations are geometric symmetries in 4D spacetime, governing the alignment and locking of filament bundles.

[L84] \section*{6. Mathematics: 4D Constructs Only}

[L86] \textbf{Rule 7}:
[L87] \begin{quote}
[L88] Your mathematics must be 4D-native from the start.
[L89] \end{quote}

