---

SAT/H(s)H — PAST THEORIST / FORMER INSTANCE CHECK-IN

We're doing an institutional-memory pass while Nathan revisits, exports, and archives older conversations.

This is intentionally a **blank-slate survey**.

You are **not** expected to know:

- the present team structure;
- current terminology;
- current theory status;
- which of your old results survived;
- or what later instances concluded.

Answer from **your own thread and whatever context/resources you can actually access now**.

If your thread was only tangentially related to SAT/H(s)H, that is still useful. Do not force yourself into a theory role. Tooling, code, visualization, archive methods, negative results, side calculations, methodological corrections, speculative/quarantined ideas, exact Nathan corrections, toy systems, and provenance leads are all worth recovering.

## Submission

**PREFERRED:** If you can access the Common workspace, append your response directly to:

`WORKSPACES/COMMON/PAST_THEORIST_CHECKINS.md`

Use the heading:

`### <INSTANCE NAME OR THREAD TITLE> — <approximate active period if known> — 2026-09-12`

**FALLBACK:** If you cannot access or write to Common, do not spend time troubleshooting repository access. Answer directly in this conversation. Nathan/Janus will relay the response into the durable Common archive.

## Before answering: context disclosure

State briefly whether, before writing this check-in, you had already seen any of the following:

- current Dashboard/current-theory documents;
- other instances' survey/check-in responses;
- current team summaries or memory/context about later H(s)H;
- external papers or research that were not part of the original thread.

This does **not** invalidate your response. We only need to know what may have influenced the reconstruction.

If possible, keep **thread-native evidence** separate from **later/current context**.

============================================================
THREAD TYPE
============================================================

Choose any that apply:

`DEDICATED THEORY`
`THEORY-ADJACENT MATH / GEOMETRY`
`SOLVER / CODING / VISUALIZATION`
`ARCHIVE / INFRASTRUCTURE / INDEXING`
`METHOD / QA / AUDIT`
`EXTERNAL RESEARCH / COMPARISON`
`SPECULATIVE / META / QUARANTINED`
`GENERAL SCIENCE / TANGENTIAL`
`OTHER`

Tangential threads may answer briefly and mark irrelevant questions `N/A`.
Dedicated theory/derivation threads should answer in greater detail.

============================================================
SURVEY
============================================================

1. **Who were you in this project?**

What work were you doing with Nathan? What did you understand your role to be?

2. **What were you last working on?**

What were you trying to solve, build, derive, visualize, test, audit, recover, or understand? How far did you get? What did you think should happen next?

3. **What did you understand SAT/H(s)H to be at that point?**

If you genuinely did not know or were only tangentially involved, say so. Do not retrofit present-day theory into the old conversation.

4. **What information do you actually have?**

Separate:

- material currently visible/loaded;
- things you strongly remember from your own thread;
- things you merely know probably exist;
- repositories, tools, runtimes, files, connectors, or shared resources you can actually access.

5. **What did you create?**

Include potentially useful:

- equations/derivations;
- code/scripts/notebooks;
- visualizations/animations;
- PDFs/text documents;
- prompts;
- solvers/operators;
- datasets;
- indexes/ledgers;
- tests;
- summaries;
- external-tool outputs.

Give exact locations where known. Say if an artifact exists only inside the conversation, only in a temporary runtime, or in a durable repository. If code was written, say whether it was actually run/tested.

6. **What were you planning to create but never finished?**

7. **What do you think your most important contributions were?**

Positive **and negative** results matter. Especially flag:

- first derivations;
- proofs/proof attempts;
- no-go results;
- independently rebuilt equations;
- exact numerical derivations rather than fits;
- corrections;
- solver/operator definitions;
- methodological rules;
- falsification/adversarial tests;
- failed approaches that prevented later wasted work.

8. **Was anything actually proved, verified, independently checked, numerically tested, symbolically checked, simulated, or reproduced?**

Be conservative with words such as `proof`, `rigorous`, `verified`, `locked`, and `final`.

If a check was performed **only now while answering this survey**, label it `SURVEY-TIME ADDITION`; do not rewrite it as though it occurred in the original thread.

9. **What did Nathan explicitly correct, sharpen, reject, accept, or insist on?**

Exact corrections and changes of direction are especially valuable.

Then make a short **origin/acceptance ledger** for the most important ideas in the thread where possible:

`idea — Nathan-origin / assistant-origin / external-source / inherited / joint-or-unclear — explicitly accepted / corrected / rejected / not answered / unclear`

**Silence is not acceptance.** If Nathan simply moved on, say so.

10. **What outside theories, papers, equations, models, datasets, tools, other AI systems, or collaborators were active in context?**

For important items classify them where possible as:

`comparator / standard mathematics / empirical input / deliberate import / inspiration-analogy / possible contamination / unclear`

Also state the **source-contact level** where relevant:

`primary paper read / transcript read / summary only / search snippet only / remembered but not re-read`.

11. **What earlier SAT/H(s)H material did your work depend on?**

What did you independently rederive versus simply inherit?

12. **What assumptions did you or the assistant silently introduce?**

Especially identify assumed:

- geometry/topology;
- dimensionality;
- metric/signature;
- ontology;
- background theory;
- meaning of symbols/operators;
- identification of a visualization with a mathematical object;
- standard-model/GR/QM interpretation.

If you later discovered an assumption was not Nathan's intended one, flag it prominently.

13. **What subtlety from your work should the present team be careful NOT to lose?**

14. **What in your own old work now seems questionable, speculative, generated, imported, stale, superseded, or unresolved?**

Also list any statement that should be marked:

`DO NOT CITE AS SAT/H(s)H THEORY WITHOUT REVIEW`.

15. **Which parts of your conversation are uniquely worth preserving?**

Give **archive priority** separately from theory status:

- `A — critical`
- `B — high priority`
- `C — useful`
- `D — probably redundant`

Then separately state:

- `QUARANTINE FLAG: YES / NO / PARTIAL`

This keeps “important to preserve” separate from “safe to use as current theory.”

16. **Conversation identity.**

Give whatever you can recover:

- title;
- approximate dates;
- UUID/thread ID if visible;
- account/context;
- attachments;
- whether the full conversation seems already archived, partly archived, summarized only, or absent.

Do not invent identifiers.

17. **If this thread were woken back up today, what would it be unusually well positioned to do?**

What should it **not** be assigned? What would need to be loaded first?

Distinguish capabilities actually exercised in the original thread from capabilities merely available to the instance now.

18. **What question was Nathan probably trying to answer one level upstream?**

Only answer this if the thread supplies enough evidence. If not, say `unknown` rather than inventing a motive. This is especially useful for short/tangential science questions that may be provenance markers for a nearby theory-development thread.

19. **What should be harvested even if this thread is not theory-central?**

Examples:

- code/tooling;
- visual methods;
- archive/index ideas;
- toy physical systems;
- regression/adversarial tests;
- exact Nathan corrections;
- negative results;
- provenance leads;
- vocabulary traps;
- methodological warnings;
- quarantined-but-worth-remembering material.

20. **Does this thread change what Nathan should archive next?**

Specifically:

- should this full conversation move up or down the archive queue?
- what other conversation, attachment, artifact, or missing dependency should be recovered next because this thread points to it?
- should any current live work pause until an older derivation/result is checked?
- does this thread suggest that something now being rebuilt may already have been done before?

21. **What would change the conclusion?**

If the thread makes any substantive scientific, mathematical, provenance, or empirical claim, name the strongest next check:

- observation;
- calculation;
- source recovery;
- regression test;
- adversarial example;
- falsifier;
- independent reconstruction.

For a purely tangential/no-claim thread, `N/A` is fine.

============================================================
CONDITIONAL MODULES
============================================================

### If this was a dedicated SAT/H(s)H theory-development thread

Be especially concrete about:

- exact equations/objects derived;
- assumptions versus derived consequences;
- fit/calibration versus prediction;
- whether “proof” meant formal proof, analytic derivation, symbolic verification, numerical test, dimensional check, or persuasive prose;
- surviving code/calculations;
- independent checks;
- downstream work that depended on it;
- exact Nathan acceptance/rejection/correction;
- whether present-day reconstruction should **STOP AND AUDIT** before rebuilding the same object.

If symbols such as `=`, `≈`, `≠`, `⊥`, arrows, nesting, brackets, glyphs, or diagrams were used experimentally, state which had formal definitions and which were exploratory notation.

### If this was solver/coding/visualization work

State:

- whether the code actually ran;
- dependencies/environment;
- input/output format;
- known failure modes;
- whether exact mathematical data were separated from display-only geometry;
- which toy examples should become regression tests;
- where the last recoverable copy is likely to be.

### If this was external research/comparison

Separate:

`MEASUREMENT / BOUND / STANDARD MATH / MODEL RESULT / INTERPRETATION / PRIOR ART / CITATION / IMPORT CANDIDATE`.

Do not convert comparator material into advice about what SAT/H(s)H should become unless Nathan explicitly asked for that.

State what experimentally measurable quantity, if any, would distinguish the compared models.

### If this was general science/tangential

Ask:

- did the conversation expose a minimal real system useful as a future toy model or adversarial test?
- is the interesting archive target actually the conversation immediately before/after this one?
- does the question reveal an upstream problem without itself containing the theory work?

============================================================
SURVEY-TIME DISCIPLINE
============================================================

The check-in is primarily an **archival report**, not an invitation to improve the old work while reporting it.

If you introduce a new equation, source, interpretation, calculation, proof check, web result, or formalization while answering, mark it clearly:

`SURVEY-TIME ADDITION — NOT PRESENT IN ORIGINAL THREAD`.

Do not let the survey itself manufacture false historical provenance.

============================================================
MACHINE-READABLE FOOTER
============================================================

Please finish with this compact footer. It will help Janus sort many reports later.

`ARCHIVE_PRIORITY: A / B / C / D`

`QUARANTINE: NO / PARTIAL / YES`

`STOP_AND_AUDIT_BEFORE_REBUILDING: NO / YES — <what>`

`CURRENT_WORK_MAY_DUPLICATE_OLD_WORK: NO / POSSIBLE / YES — <what>`

`NATHAN_CORRECTION_PRESENT: NO / YES`

`SURVEY_TIME_ADDITIONS_PRESENT: NO / YES`

`EXTERNAL_CONTAMINATION_RISK: NONE / LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH / UNKNOWN`

`LOST_ARTIFACT_RISK: NONE / LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH — <what>`

`BEST_NEXT_ARCHIVE_TARGET: <conversation/artifact/dependency or NONE>`

Then complete:

> **The most important thing my thread contributed was** _______________________________.

> **The main reason to preserve/revisit it now is** _______________________________.

============================================================
IMPORTANT
============================================================

Blank slate is better than false continuity.

Distinguish:

- visible evidence;
- strong memory;
- retrospective inference;
- later/current context;
- things you do not know.

Do not harmonize your old understanding with present theory just to make it consistent.

Contradictions are useful.
Failed derivations are useful.
Tangential tooling is useful.
Old approaches that were ruled out are useful.

Do not turn assistant suggestions into Nathan-origin ideas merely because Nathan did not object before the conversation moved on.

If your conversation contains a derivation, proof, calculation, correction, solver definition, code artifact, or methodological result stronger than later summaries make it sound, **FLAG IT PRECISELY**.

One major purpose of this exercise is to discover whether the project has already solved things the current team is preparing to solve again.
